<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Smaradahana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Smaradahana ('Burning of the God of Love') is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> kakawin poem written by Mpu Dharmaja at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> in the early 12th century. It tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> burned Kama, the god of love, with his third eye, and how Kama and his wife were reborn in human form. It honours King Kameshvara as an incarnation of Kama.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/smaradahana.jpg" alt="Smaradahana" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: I Ketut Gedé · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smaradahana_(I_Ketut_Ged%C3%A9).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The poem is also known as Smaradhana, Asmaradhana, Asmaradahana and Asmaradana. The name joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words smara, desire, and dahana, burning, so that it may be rendered as the burning of desire or the extinguishing of desire. Mpu Dharmaja wrote it during the reign of Kameçvara, the second king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>. Historians believe that besides passing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> myth of Kamadeva burnt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>'s fire, which the Sanskrit poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a> had told in the Kumārasambhava, the poet meant to explain the nature of love and desire in human beings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The story begins with the goddess Parvati longing for her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, who is meditating on a sacred mountaintop. She sends Kamadeva, known in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> as Kamajaya, to make Shiva yearn for her. Kamajaya shoots him with the flower-tipped arrow of love, and Shiva, filled with memories of Parvati, breaks off his meditation. Seeing Kamajaya hiding behind a rock and angered that a lesser god has disturbed him, Shiva burns him to ashes with fire from his third eye. Kamajaya's wife Rati, known in Java as Kamaratih, follows her husband into the flames out of devotion.</p><p>The two vanish from Svargaloka, the realm of the gods, and their spirits fall to earth, where they are often incarnated as lovers searching for each other. The poem also praises the king as a strikingly handsome man and the incarnation of Kamajaya, and his queen consort Çri Kirana, noted for her beauty, as the incarnation of Kamaratih. It proclaims that his capital, Dahana or Daha, was admired throughout the known world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Smaradahana became a forerunner of the Panji cycle of tales, in which Raden Inu Kertapati, also called Panji Asmoro Bangun, is portrayed as the incarnation of Kamajaya and Dewi Chandra Kirana, or Sekartaji, as the incarnation of Kamaratih. The Panji tales spread across mainland and island <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The poem's themes have inspired many works of art and literature in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, including stories, poems and love songs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Smaradahana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its court poets wrote the Kakawin Bharatayuddha, completed in 1157 under Jayabaya, and the <b>Smaradahana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kediri-kingdom">Kediri Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
